## 5. F-3 — CLOSED

  scripts/safe-item-path-mutants.mjs now carries per-mutant kills:
    mA `1. safeItemPath(".")`, `1. safeItemPath("..")`
    mB `1. safeItemPath("../W-001")`
    mC `1. safeItemPath("/etc/passwd")`

  Pairwise disjoint as sets. I also checked the substring-collision question the
  brief's red-team section raises, since these labels nest by construction:
    `1. safeItemPath(".")`  is NOT a substring of `1. safeItemPath("..")`
      — the needle needs `("."` followed by `)`; the haystack has `("..")`.
    `1. safeItemPath("..")` is NOT a substring of `1. safeItemPath("../W-001")`
      or of `1. safeItemPath("../../etc/passwd")` — `/` follows, not `"`.
    `1. safeItemPath("../W-001")` is a prefix of nothing else in the table.
  The prefixing design survives contact.

  DISCRIMINATION IS REAL, not just distinct spelling. From the committed 01.log,
  mA's complete FAIL set is
    "", ".", "..", "..\\..\\win.ini", "C:\\Windows\\win.ini"   (5 lines)
  It contains neither `../W-001` (mB's label) nor `/etc/passwd` (mC's label) — 0
  occurrences of each. So mB's and mC's labels are assertions mA demonstrably cannot
  produce, and the round-1 situation (one label crediting three mutants) is gone.
  The rationale committed alongside the constants is correct on the mechanism: mA
  leaves the dirname check standing, so it is observable only on rows that introduce
  no separator; mB is the only contract mutant that removes the dirname check too.

